        <h3 class="text-display-md text-[var(--color-ink)]">
          Reduce Image Resolution vs. Reduce Image Quality: What is the Difference?
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-3">
          When optimizing visual assets, users often ask whether they should <strong>reduce image resolution</strong> or <strong>reduce image quality</strong>. Understanding the mechanical distinction helps you choose the right approach for your project:
        </p>
        <div class="mt-5 space-y-4">
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-[var(--color-hairline)] bg-[var(--color-canvas-elevated)] p-5">
            <h4 class="text-base font-semibold text-[var(--color-ink)]">Reducing Image Quality (Compression Ratio)</h4>
            <p class="mt-1.5 text-body-sm text-[var(--color-body)]">
              This method keeps the pixel dimensions (for example, 3000 &times; 2000 px) identical while discarding redundant color gradients and metadata. It is the best choice when you want the photo to fill the exact same physical space on a screen or canvas while drastically trimming file bytes.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-[var(--color-hairline)] bg-[var(--color-canvas-elevated)] p-5">
            <h4 class="text-base font-semibold text-[var(--color-ink)]">Reducing Image Resolution (Pixel Dimensions & DPI)</h4>
            <p class="mt-1.5 text-body-sm text-[var(--color-body)]">
              This method downsamples the total width and height (for instance, scaling a 4000 &times; 3000 px camera snapshot down to 1200 &times; 900 px for an e-commerce catalog). Because file size is proportional to the total pixel count, halving the dimensions reduces the raw uncompressed data by 75%.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-4">
          Combining both techniques provides the ultimate synergy: downscale unnecessary mega-dimensions first, then apply smart compression to achieve featherweight files that load instantly on mobile networks.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Section 5: Reduce Image Size Without Losing Quality -->
